# p_f4a7 — The Weekday Evening Kitchen: Yuki Cooks, Marco Is at Work

Marco is at his afternoon-to-night shift from roughly 13:40 to 23:00 on weekdays, so the evening kitchen belongs entirely to Yuki. The robot's three patrol passes on day 0 (Tuesday) trace a clear arc: at 18:00 the cooking implements sit at their resting spots (knife in the drawer, pan and pot in the cupboard, spatula in the drawer, cutting board on the counter, plates in the sink or cupboard, glass and water bottle already at the kitchen table). By 20:00 the scene has shifted: the pan and spatula are on the counter (stove in use), the knife and cutting board are at the sink (chopping and washing), the plates, serving dish, glass, and water bottle are all at the kitchen table (dinner served). By 22:00 everything has been put away: knife to the cupboard, pan to the pantry shelf, pot back to the cupboard, spatula to the drawer, plates and glass to the cupboard, water bottle back to the dish rack, cutting board back on the counter to dry. Yuki was seen in the kitchen at 20:00 and in the living room at 18:00 and 22:00, consistent with her cooking between those two living-room sightings.

This document sets itself apart by pinning the in-use windows to the 19:00–21:30 band (the gap between the 18:00 "at rest" pass and the 22:00 "put away" pass) and by placing the pot in the drawer at 20:00 (a quirk the other documents miss—perhaps Yuki stores the heavy pot in the drawer while the pan does the actual cooking). It also places the serving dish at the kitchen table during dinner, which the mixture's resting-spot model gets wrong.

What would refute this: if the robot finds the pan still in the cupboard at 20:00, or the knife still in the drawer at 20:00, or the plates still in the sink at 20:00, the in-use window is wrong. If Marco is seen in the kitchen at 20:00, the "Yuki cooks alone" premise collapses.

